Comprehensive Technical Reference & Hardware Integration Guide
Microgrid Telemetry Processing
Microgrid telemetry processing involves capturing, streaming, and acting upon high-frequency operational data from distributed energy resources (DERs)—such as solar arrays, battery storage banks, generators, and smart meters. Unlike traditional enterprise software, microgrid systems demand deterministic performance, low latency, and resilience to intermittent network connectivity at the edge.
Key Technical Components
•	Protocol Gateways: Hardware and software nodes communicate using industrial standards like Modbus TCP/RTU, MQTT, or OPC-UA. Your ingestion layer must reliably translate raw hardware registers into structured application events.
•	High-Frequency Stream Processing: Metrics such as voltage, current, phase angle, frequency, and battery state-of-charge (SoC) update continuously. Processing these streams requires handling rapid data spikes without thread starvation.
•	Time-Series Storage: Telemetry is inherently temporal. High-throughput pipelines rely on time-series databases optimized for massive write volumes, automatic retention policies, and efficient downsampling.
•	Closed-Loop Control: Telemetry data isn't just for passive visualization; it feeds automated optimization engines that trigger load shedding, manage peak shaving, or adjust inverter setpoints in real time.
Implementing Telemetry Pipelines in Java
Java provides a powerful ecosystem for building industrial telemetry engines due to its mature concurrency models and high-performance networking frameworks:
•	Netty / Eclipse Paho: Utilize non-blocking I/O frameworks to maintain thousands of concurrent TCP or MQTT socket connections from remote field devices.
•	Reactive Streams (Spring WebFlux / Project Reactor): Perfect for non-blocking, event-driven backpressured architectures that gracefully handle telemetry bursts.
•	Memory Management: For high-frequency edge ingestion operating at sub-second or kilohertz sampling rates, utilizing direct byte buffers minimizes Garbage Collection (GC) pauses that can compromise control loops.
Overview of the ESP32 Microcontroller
An ESP32 is a tiny, incredibly powerful, and inexpensive microcontroller chip created by Espressif Systems. Think of it as a modern cousin to traditional Arduino boards (like the Arduino Uno), but packed with advanced communication features right out of the box.
Key Features of the ESP32
•	Built-in Wi-Fi and Bluetooth: Unlike a standard Arduino Uno (which requires an extra shield or module to connect to the internet), every ESP32 has Wi-Fi and Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) built directly onto the chip.
•	Arduino IDE Compatibility: You can write code for the ESP32 using the familiar Arduino programming language and upload it straight through the Arduino IDE by simply installing the ESP32 board package.
•	Processing Power & Memory: It features a dual-core 32-bit processor running at up to 240 MHz, with megabytes of flash memory and RAM—making it lightyears ahead of older 8-bit microcontrollers.
•	Analog-to-Digital Converters (ADCs): It includes multiple pins capable of reading analog voltages, which is essential for measuring the exact voltage drop of your AA or AAA batteries.
Why the ESP32 Shines for Battery Monitoring: If you are building an AA/AAA battery monitoring setup, an ESP32 gives you a major advantage over a wired Arduino: wireless telemetry. Instead of connecting your Arduino to your Java application via a USB cable, an ESP32 can read the battery voltage on an analog pin, connect to your local Wi-Fi network, and transmit that data straight to your Java application over the network (using protocols like MQTT, HTTP, or WebSockets).
Hardware Wiring Guide: AA/AAA Batteries to ESP32
Connecting your AA/AAA battery holder to the ESP32 is straightforward. Since a single fresh alkaline battery sits at around 1.5V (and dips down to ~1.0V when dead), it fits comfortably within the ESP32's 0V to 3.3V analog input range.
Option 1: Measuring a Single Battery (1.5V Max)
If you are testing just one AA or AAA battery at a time, you can wire it directly without any resistors:
•	Battery Positive (+) -> ESP32 Analog Pin (e.g., GPIO 34)
•	Battery Negative (-) -> ESP32 GND (Ground)
Note: Make sure your ESP32 and the battery share a common ground if they are powered by separate sources.
Option 2: Measuring a Multi-Battery Pack (2 or 3 batteries in series = 3V to 4.5V+)
If your battery holder contains multiple batteries wired in series, the total voltage can exceed the ESP32's 3.3V max limit, which can risk damaging the pin. To safely measure higher voltages, use a simple voltage divider made of two resistors to cut the voltage in half.
The Resistor Setup:
•	Use two identical resistors (for example, 10k ohms each).
•	Resistor 1: Connected between the Battery Positive (+) and the ESP32 Analog Pin (GPIO 34).
•	Resistor 2: Connected between the ESP32 Analog Pin (GPIO 34) and ESP32 GND.
•	Battery Negative (-) -> ESP32 GND.
Adjusting the Code for Option 2: Because your resistors cut the voltage in half before it hits the ESP32 pin, you just need to multiply the final reading by 2.0 in your Arduino code.
Handling Both Single Cells and Multi-Battery Packs (Advanced Dual-Pin Setup)
Handling both single cells (1.5V) and multi-battery packs (3V to 4.5V+) on the same ESP32 requires a smart approach because the voltage range changes. The cleanest way to handle this is by using two separate analog pins with specific wiring for each, and exposing two different endpoints in your ESP32 web server so your Android app can check whichever one is plugged in.
•	Step 1: The Dual-Pin Wiring Strategy
•	Single Battery Setup: Battery Positive -> GPIO 34 (Direct connection, no resistors needed since 1.5V is well under the 3.3V limit).
•	Multi-Battery Pack Setup: Pack Positive -> Resistor 1 (10kΩ) -> GPIO 35 -> Resistor 2 (10kΩ) -> GND (Voltage divider cuts the pack voltage in half to keep it safe).
Important ESP32 ADC Note: The ESP32 analog-to-digital converter (ADC) isn't perfectly linear right at the extreme bottom (0V) and top (3.3V) ends. For the best accuracy when tracking battery health between 1.0V and 1.5V, GPIO 34, GPIO 35, GPIO 36 (VP), or GPIO 39 (VN) are your best choices because they are dedicated input-only analog pins.
Configuring the ESP32 Access Point & Web Server
Configuring the ESP32 as its own Wi-Fi Access Point (AP) is a brilliant choice, especially if you want a self-contained system that works anywhere without needing a home router.
Step 1: The ESP32 Access Point Code (Arduino C/C++ - Single & Multi-Pack Endpoints)
This unified code sets up two distinct web addresses on your ESP32 hotspot: 192.168.4.1/battery/single and 192.168.4.1/battery/pack.
#include <WiFi.h>
#include <WebServer.h>

const char* ssid = "ESP32_Battery_Monitor";
const char* password = "password123";

WebServer server(80);

const int singlePin = 34; // Direct connection for single battery
const int packPin = 35;   // Voltage divider connection for multi-pack

void handleSingleBattery() {
  int sensorValue = analogRead(singlePin);
  float voltage = sensorValue * (3.3 / 4095.0);
  server.send(200, "text/plain", String(voltage));
}

void handlePackBattery() {
  int sensorValue = analogRead(packPin);
  float voltage = sensorValue * (3.3 / 4095.0) * 2.0;
  server.send(200, "text/plain", String(voltage));
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(1000);
  
  WiFi.softAP(ssid, password);
  IPAddress myIP = WiFi.softAPIP();
  Serial.print("AP IP address: ");
  Serial.println(myIP);

  server.on("/battery/single", handleSingleBattery);
  server.on("/battery/pack", handlePackBattery);
  
  server.begin();
  Serial.println("HTTP server started in AP mode.");
}

void loop() {
  server.handleClient();
}
Based on the provided document, the output from the ESP32 depends on how it is accessed:
•	Access Point Initialization: When the ESP32 first boots up and starts its Wi-Fi Access Point (AP) mode, it prints the AP IP address to the Serial Monitor:
•	AP IP address: 192.168.4.1
•	HTTP Server Startup: Once the web server begins running, it prints:
•	HTTP server started in AP mode.
•	HTTP Request Endpoints (/battery/single or /battery/pack): When an Android app or client makes a GET request to these endpoints, the ESP32 outputs the calculated voltage as plain text strings (for example, 1.45 or 3.20).
Android App Implementation (Java)
When using an ESP32 Access Point, the workflow on your phone is:
1. Open your phone's Wi-Fi settings and connect to ESP32_Battery_Monitor (using password password123).
2. Open your Android app and hit the button to fetch the voltage (either single cell or multi-pack).
import android.os.Bundle;
import android.widget.Button;
import android.widget.TextView;
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.net.HttpURLConnection;
import java.net.URL;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    private TextView tvBatteryStatus;
    private Button btnCheckSingle, btnCheckPack;
    
    private final String urlSingle = "http://192.168.4.1/battery/single";
    private final String urlPack = "http://192.168.4.1/battery/pack";

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);
        
        tvBatteryStatus = findViewById(R.id.tvBatteryStatus);
        btnCheckSingle = findViewById(R.id.btnCheckSingle);
        btnCheckPack = findViewById(R.id.btnCheckPack);
        
        btnCheckSingle.setOnClickListener(v -> fetchVoltage(urlSingle, "Single Cell"));
        btnCheckPack.setOnClickListener(v -> fetchVoltage(urlPack, "Multi-Pack"));
    }

    private void fetchVoltage(String endpoint, String label) {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();
        executor.execute(() -> {
            try {
                URL url = new URL(endpoint);
                HttpURLConnection conn = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection();
                conn.setRequestMethod("GET");
                conn.setConnectTimeout(3000);
                
                BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(conn.getInputStream()));
                StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder();
                String line;
                while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                    stringBuilder.append(line);
                }
                reader.close();
                
                String voltageResult = stringBuilder.toString();
                runOnUiThread(() -> tvBatteryStatus.setText(label + " Voltage: " + voltageResult + "V"));
            } catch (Exception e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
                runOnUiThread(() -> tvBatteryStatus.setText("Failed to read " + label + ". Check Wi-Fi connection."));
            }
        });
    }
}

Standard Java Implementation to Check Battery Status
Standard software languages like Java run on computer operating systems (like Windows, macOS, Linux, or Android) and cannot directly interface with physical AA or AAA batteries unless connected via specialized external hardware (such as an Arduino, Raspberry Pi, or a USB-connected multimeter/microcontroller). Computers and phones running Java typically track device-level power (like a laptop's internal lithium-ion battery pack or an Android phone's battery).
Scenario A: Your Java program runs on a computer reading a host power source
If you are trying to read the battery status of the computer running your code (e.g., Windows via JNA):
import com.sun.jna.Native;
import com.sun.jna.Structure;
import com.sun.jna.platform.win32.Kernel32;
import com.sun.jna.platform.win32.WinDef;

public class BatteryChecker {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Kernel32.SYSTEM_POWER_STATUS batteryStatus = new Kernel32.SYSTEM_POWER_STATUS();
        Kernel32.INSTANCE.GetSystemPowerStatus(batteryStatus);
        System.out.println("Battery Life Percent: " + batteryStatus.BatteryLifePercent + "%");
        System.out.println("AC Line Status (Plugged in): " + batteryStatus.ACLineStatus);
    }
}
Scenario B: You are using an Arduino / Microcontroller with AA/AAA batteries attached to hardware
If you have AA/AAA batteries powering an external circuit and want Java to read data sent from that hardware over a Serial/USB port (using a library like com.fazecast.jSerialComm), the architecture looks like this:
1. Microcontroller Code (Arduino C++): Reads the analog voltage pin where the AA/AAA batteries are connected and prints it to Serial.
2. Java Code: Listens to the Serial port and parses the voltage.
import com.fazecast.jSerialComm.SerialPort;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class AABatteryMonitor {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SerialPort port = SerialPort.getCommPorts()[0]; // Change index as needed
        port.setComPortParameters(9600, 8, 1, 0);
        port.setComPortTimeouts(SerialPort.TIMEOUT_READ_SEMI_BLOCKING, 0, 0);
        
        if (port.openPort()) {
            System.out.println("Connected to hardware monitor successfully.");
            try (Scanner scanner = new Scanner(port.getInputStream())) {
                while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
                    String rawData = scanner.nextLine();
                    parseBatteryVoltage(rawData);
                }
            }
        } else {
            System.out.println("Failed to open serial port.");
        }
    }

    public static void parseBatteryVoltage(String data) {
        try {
            double voltage = Double.parseDouble(data.trim());
            String health = "Unknown";
            if (voltage >= 1.4) health = "Full Fresh";
            else if (voltage >= 1.2) health = "Good";
            else if (voltage >= 1.0) health = "Low";
            else health = "Dead / Replace";
            System.out.printf("Voltage: %.2fV | Status: %s%n", voltage, health);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            // Ignore non-numeric debug lines from serial
        }
    }
}


No, alkaline and most common household batteries do not stay at a constant 1.5V until suddenly dropping to zero.
As noted in the document, a single fresh alkaline battery starts out sitting at around 1.5V, but it gradually dips down to about 1.0V as it discharges its chemical energy.
Here is why voltage changes progressively rather than instantly:
 * Discharge Curve: As a battery is used, the chemical reactants inside are consumed, and its internal resistance increases. This causes a steady, sloping drop in voltage over time rather than a flat line.
 * Health Thresholds: Because voltage drops gradually, systems use thresholds (like the ones in the Java code: \ge 1.4\text{V} for “Full Fresh”, down to < 1.0\text{V} for “Dead”) to determine how much life is actually left.
 * Load Impact: When current is actively being drawn from a battery (under load), the voltage sags even further depending on how hard the device is working.


